.TH HSH 1 "January 2026" "hsh 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hsh \- Simple UNIX command interpreter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ./hsh
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hsh
is a simple UNIX command language interpreter that reads commands from either standard input or from a file. It is designed to emulate the basic functionality of the \fBsh\fR shell.

Upon startup, the \fBhsh\fR shell looks for commands in the directories specified by the \fBPATH\fR environment variable, or accepts absolute/relative paths to executables.

.SH INVOCATION
.B hsh
can be invoked in two modes:

.B Interactive Mode:
.br
When \fBhsh\fR is invoked without arguments and standard input is connected to a terminal, it enters interactive mode. It displays the prompt \fB($) \fR and waits for a user command.
.br
Example:
.br
.sp
.B ($) /bin/ls
.br
hsh main.c shell.c

.B Non-interactive Mode:
.br
When \fBhsh\fR is invoked with standard input connected to a pipe or file, or (optionally) with a file argument, it runs in non-interactive mode. No prompt is displayed, and commands are executed line by line until \fBEOF\fR is reached.
.br
Example:
.br
.sp
.B echo "/bin/ls" | ./hsh
.br
hsh main.c shell.c

.SH ENVIRONMENT
The shell uses the \fBPATH\fR environment variable to locate commands that are not entered as absolute or relative paths.

.SH BUILT-IN COMMANDS
The following commands are built into the shell:

.TP
.B exit
Exits the shell.

.TP
.B env
Prints the current environment variables.

.TP
.B cd [directory]
Changes the current working directory to the directory specified. If no directory is specified, the behavior depends on implementation (often changes to HOME).
(Note: Included based on the presence of \fBchdir\fR in allowed functions).

.SH ERROR HANDLING
If an executable cannot be found or executed, \fBhsh\fR prints an error message to standard error (stderr). The error format mimics \fB/bin/sh\fR:

.B ./hsh: 1: command_name: not found

Where:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB./hsh\fR is the name of the shell (argv[0]).
.IP \(bu 2
\fB1\fR is the line number.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcommand_name\fR is the command that failed.
.RE

.SH RETURN VALUE
.B hsh
returns the exit status of the last executed command. If a syntax error occurs, it returns a non-zero status.

.SH EXAMPLES
.B Interactive usage:
.sp
    $ ./hsh
    ($) /bin/ls -l
    total 20
    -rwxr-xr-x 1 user user 18000 Jan 1 12:00 hsh
    -rw-r--r-- 1 user user   500 Jan 1 12:00 main.c
    ($) exit
    $

.B Piping usage:
.sp
    $ echo "/bin/ls" | ./hsh
    hsh main.c
    $

